# Copy to .env and fill in — or just run scripts/bootstrap.sh, which writes .env
# from this file with a generated gateway token, and never overwrites an existing
# one. .env is gitignored and excluded from the Docker build context by
# .dockerignore, so nothing here is ever baked into an image layer.
#
# ===========================================================================
# TWO PATHS. PICK ONE. THE RECOMMENDED ONE PUTS NO SECRET VALUE IN THIS FILE.
# ===========================================================================
#
# RECOMMENDED — file-backed secrets (and the only path that works on Azure):
#
#     scripts/init-file-secrets.sh          # creates secrets/runtime/* (0600)
#     $EDITOR secrets/runtime/*             # paste each value into its own file
#     export COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.secrets.yaml
#
#   Every credential — Pretorin key, Slack tokens, gateway token, model-provider
#   key — is delivered the same way: one file per secret, mounted read-only, with
#   only the services that need it receiving it. THE COMPLETE FILE CHECKLIST AND
#   THE PER-SERVICE DELIVERY MATRIX LIVE IN docs/file-secrets.md — that is the one
#   place to look, and this file deliberately does not duplicate it.
#
#   On this path .env holds NON-SECRETS ONLY: SLACK_CHANNEL_ID, the model-provider
#   selection note below, and the host-side GitHub App identifiers. Leave every
#   secret variable in the LEGACY section empty.
#
# LEGACY — secret values in this file. Still supported for existing local
#   deployments, and still what scripts/bootstrap.sh writes. The cost is real and
#   is the reason it is not recommended: compose's env_file hands every value here
#   to the container AND to `docker inspect`, so anyone who can query the daemon
#   can read them. See docs/plans/phase3.md.
#
# Either way the rendered ~/.openclaw/openclaw.json holds the literal ${NAME} and
# never the value — OpenClaw substitutes it at config load time, per process.

# THE ACCESS-CONTROL BOUNDARY. Pretorin enforces this key's scopes server-side,
# and Compliance Claw deliberately does not second-guess it: the full Pretorin
# MCP tool surface is registered, and what the agent can actually do is whatever
# this key authorizes.
#   empty         -> the gateway still starts, but every platform tool fails
#   read-only     -> write tools are rejected by the platform
#   write-enabled -> the agent can write to the platform
#
# A READ-ONLY KEY IS SUFFICIENT for everything Compliance Claw does: bootstrap,
# onboarding (scripts/onboard-targets.sh) and assessment all work with one,
# because binding source resolvers, verifying them and provisioning recipes write
# only host-local state — the single platform call is a READ of the recommended
# source-kind profile. Sufficient is not the same as imposed: this deployment does
# not default you to a restricted posture, it just does not need more than one.
#
# The key is operator-configurable, and a write-enabled key is an INTENTIONAL
# OPT-IN for platform writes: evidence upload, control status, risks, vendors.
# Nothing here escalates on its own. On a shared channel every user inherits this
# key's authority with no per-user attribution, so a write-enabled key needs
# user-level attribution and RBAC first.
PRETORIN_API_KEY=

# THE KEY'S OWN SCOPES ARE THE ONLY THING THAT DECIDES READ VS WRITE. Compliance
# Claw applies no local permission filtering and has no "mode" to set: a read-only
# key gets platform rejections on write tools, a write-enabled key does not, and
# both are fully supported. Nothing here defaults you to a locally-restricted
# posture, because nothing here enforces one.
#
# If you are upgrading and your existing .env has a key-mode line, DELETE IT —
# nothing reads it any more. It was never configuration: it only told
# scripts/smoke.sh what a deliberate write probe SHOULD do, since `pretorin
# whoami` reports authentication but not scopes. That belongs to the test harness,
# so it is now a smoke-test flag (--expect-read-only / --test-write-enabled) and
# not a deployment setting. Leaving the old line in place is harmless but does
# nothing.

# Gateway auth. Required: the gateway exits 1 without it. This is the single
# authoritative token source — there is deliberately no ~/.openclaw/gateway.token
# to drift against it. scripts/bootstrap.sh generates one with
# `openssl rand -hex 32` when it creates .env.
OPENCLAW_GATEWAY_TOKEN=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MODEL PROVIDER — one credential, and NO variable in this file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The agent needs a model. Populate EXACTLY ONE of these files:
#
#     secrets/runtime/openai-api-key        (matches the baked-in default model)
#     secrets/runtime/anthropic-api-key
#
# Both are created empty by scripts/init-file-secrets.sh; leave the other alone.
# They are mounted ONLY into the `openclaw` gateway service, because that is the
# only place agent turns run — the Pretorin CLI never needs a model credential.
#
# NOTHING ELSE TO SET. The entrypoint picks the OpenAI request adapter from
# whether an OpenAI key is present, on every start, so the documented startup
# order works without a config edit. (The wrong adapter is the failure where the
# key authenticates and then every turn fails.)
#
# The default model is baked at build time (MODEL in versions.env). If you
# populate the Anthropic file instead, also select a matching model once:
#     docker compose run --rm cli openclaw models set anthropic/<model>
#     docker compose restart openclaw
#
# The device-code login path (`openclaw models auth login --device-code`) remains
# supported and needs no key file at all; see README.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SLACK — optional. Leave all three empty and Slack is simply not used.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Set up: import slack/app-manifest.json at api.slack.com/apps/new (do not
# assemble scopes by hand), then follow the steps in that file's header.
#
# ALL THREE ARE REQUIRED TOGETHER. Two of three is refused with a message naming
# the missing one, because a bot that connects but belongs to no channel — or a
# channel allowlist with no tokens — looks like broken software rather than
# missing configuration.
#
# These must be in .env BEFORE the first `docker compose up`. Config is seeded
# once and never overwritten, so adding them to an already-running deployment does
# nothing until you reset with `docker compose down -v`. The container warns
# explicitly when it detects exactly that situation.
#
# Slack connects OUTBOUND over a websocket (Socket Mode). The gateway needs no
# public URL and no inbound port: compose still publishes only 127.0.0.1:18789.
#
# ############################################################################
# # SECURITY — READ BEFORE PUTTING A WRITE-ENABLED PRETORIN KEY HERE.        #
# #                                                                          #
# # A shared Slack channel means EVERY MEMBER OF THAT CHANNEL acts with the   #
# # authority of the PRETORIN_API_KEY above, and there is NO PER-USER         #
# # ATTRIBUTION anywhere in the audit trail — the platform sees one key, not  #
# # the person who typed the message.                                         #
# #                                                                          #
# # The documented recommendation is therefore: A READ-ONLY KEY FOR SHARED     #
# # CHANNELS — a recommendation about which KEY to issue, not a local control. #
# # Using a write-enabled key in a shared channel is explicitly warned        #
# # against: any channel member could cause evidence, control status, risks   #
# # or vendor records to be written, and nothing would record who. Do not do  #
# # it until RBAC and user-level attribution exist. See SECURITY.md.          #
# ############################################################################
#
# LEGACY VALUE PATH — on the recommended path leave this empty and paste the
# token into secrets/runtime/slack-app-token instead.
# Basic Information -> App-Level Tokens -> Generate Token and Scopes,
# add connections:write. Starts with xapp-.
SLACK_APP_TOKEN=
# LEGACY VALUE PATH — recommended path: secrets/runtime/slack-bot-token.
# Install App -> Install to Workspace -> Bot User OAuth Token. Starts with xoxb-.
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=
# The ONE channel the agent serves, as a stable channel ID — right-click the
# channel in Slack -> Copy link -> the C... value at the end of the URL.
# A channel NAME does not work: a name-based key silently never routes, which is
# indistinguishable from the bot being broken, so the container refuses it.
# DMs are disabled and a mention is required, so the agent only ever answers when
# addressed in this channel.
SLACK_CHANNEL_ID=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRIVATE REPOSITORY TARGETS — optional. Only needed if targets.yaml marks a
# target `private: true`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The supported path is a GitHub App with ONE permission: Repository -> Contents
# -> Read-only, installed on only the repositories you want reviewed. A
# fine-grained PAT works as a development fallback but is not recommended: it
# carries a person's identity and is far harder to scope down.
#
# WHY THE KEY ITSELF IS NOT IN THIS FILE. compose's env_file hands every value
# here to the container and to `docker inspect`. A private key in .env would
# therefore be a git credential INSIDE the container, which is exactly what this
# design avoids: scripts/bootstrap.sh uses the key on the HOST to clone into
# ./workspace/targets, and the container only ever sees the resulting working
# tree over its read-only bind mount. So .env names the key's PATH; the key lives
# outside it, and both `secrets/` and `*.pem` are gitignored.
#
# Neither of the two values below is a secret.
#
# Settings -> Developer settings -> GitHub Apps -> your app -> App ID (numeric).
GITHUB_APP_ID=
# Path to the .pem you generated on that page, relative to the repository root.
# chmod 600 it.
GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=secrets/compliance-claw.private-key.pem
# Only needed if the App is installed on more than one account; otherwise the
# installation is discovered automatically.
GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=
